      {
        "title": "The dovetail joint",
        "text": "The word for peace is linked to joining things together. A carpenter's dovetail is the strongest of joints, and the book uses it as a picture of friendship with God that nothing hinders."
      },
      {
        "title": "Gold becomes currency",
        "text": "Gold does not become gold when someone discovers it, but discovery makes it something you can spend. In the same way, our union with God is already true, and becoming aware of it lets us live it."
      },
      {
        "title": "A treasure in a clay jar",
        "text": "Paul's 'earthen vessel' (2 Cor 4:7) is likened to a shell carrying a pearl. The book suggests a link between the Greek word and 'oyster', though the word more simply means pottery or shell. The plain container can never compete with what it holds. There is far more to a person than meets the eye."
      },
      {
        "title": "A midnight party",
        "text": "The man asking for three loaves at midnight is read playfully. With three friends together, why not have a party? He has the wine but has run out of bread. The point is that he got what he needed because he was not ashamed to ask."
      }
