    "questions": [
      {
        "q": "If God already knows what I need, why pray at all?",
        "a": "The book says God delights in our conversation and childlike trust, the way a lover enjoys being spoken to. Prayer is not information or pressure. It is two-way friendship that steadies the mind and fills it with God's peace."
      },
      {
        "q": "Does fasting make God more willing to act?",
        "a": "No. In the book's view, God's decisive act in Christ is already complete, so fasting is a longer chance to feast on his goodness, not leverage. It also notes that the 'and fasting' wording in the exorcism saying is missing from early manuscripts."
      },
      {
        "q": "Should we pray for revival?",
        "a": "The book thinks this is the wrong prayer. In its reading, humanity was already raised with Christ on the third day (Hosea 6:2; Eph 2:5-6), so what is needed is waking up to that, not a new move from heaven. Many Christians do still pray for revival as a fresh outpouring of the Spirit, and see no conflict between the two."
      },
      {
        "q": "What about the sinner's prayer?",
        "a": "The book observes that no one in Scripture used a 'repeat after me' formula to convert people. It thinks these formulas often trap people in cycles of trying to get assurance. Faith, in its view, is waking up to what is already true."
      },
      {
        "q": "Does God lead us into temptation?",
        "a": "James 1:13 says God tempts no one, so the book reads that line of the Lord's Prayer as asking to be kept from any hidden attachment to toil-driven, performance religion, which gives evil a foothold."
      },
      {
        "q": "How do I start when I feel nothing?",
        "a": "Start with God's nearness, not your feelings. Agree with what he says about you, then deliberately count reasons to be thankful. The book's own experience was that joy eventually wins."
      }
    ],
    "scriptures": ["Luke 6:12", "Rev 22:4", "Phil 4:4-13", "2 Tim 1:3", "Eph 6:18", "Num 13:33", "Hosea 6:2", "Eph 2:5-6", "John 7:38", "Col 3:1-3", "Psalm 50", "Isa 40:31", "Rom 8:18-28", "John 4:21-24", "2 Cor 4:13", "Ps 116", "Luke 17:21", "Rev 3:7-21", "Rev 4:1-4", "John 1:51", "Luke 11:1-14", "Col 3:16", "Heb 4:12"],
    "reflect": "If prayer starts in God's own conversation rather than in your need, what would change about the first few minutes of your next prayer?"
